Implemented Vercel Speed Insights for the Ascendra backend project.

## Changes Made:

### Created:
- None (all changes were modifications to existing files)

### Modified:
1. **backend/package.json** - Added @vercel/speed-insights dependency (v1.1.0)
2. **backend/package-lock.json** - Updated with new package and its 166 dependencies
3. **backend/app/layout.tsx** - Added SpeedInsights component from @vercel/speed-insights/next

## Implementation Details:

Following the official Vercel Speed Insights documentation (https://vercel.com/docs/speed-insights/quickstart), I implemented the Next.js-specific installation:

1. **Package Installation**: Installed @vercel/speed-insights package using npm, which added the package and its dependencies to the project.

2. **Component Integration**: Added the SpeedInsights component to the root layout file (app/layout.tsx) following Next.js App Router conventions:
   - Imported SpeedInsights from '@vercel/speed-insights/next'
   - Placed the component at the end of the <body> tag, after {children}
   - This ensures Speed Insights tracking is active on all pages

3. **Framework-Specific Approach**: Used the Next.js 13.5+ App Router approach as documented, which is the correct method for this project's architecture.

## Verification:

✅ TypeScript typecheck passed without errors
✅ Build completed successfully
✅ Tests run without failures
✅ No linting errors introduced
✅ Lock files properly updated

The Speed Insights component will automatically begin tracking performance metrics when deployed to Vercel. No additional configuration is required as the tracking script automatically deploys at `/_vercel/speed-insights/*` routes.
